estament was written in greek  um  in  you know the  constantine the emperor constantine made  christianity the official religion of  the roman empire which contributed to  its dramatic growth it's now the largest  religion in the world  uh as a result of constantine's decision  and he was he apparently spoke latin and  didn't speak greek that well although  his mother was greek  but in uh constantinople and that part  of the um  the roman empire a lot of people this be  greek maybe greek was certainly was more  common  than latin although educated romans uh  spoke greek  because it was considered the language  of of sort of culture and so forth and  and the local people spoke various  semitic languages  aramaic or phoenician i don't know  um  and that those ancient languages which  which really again i don't know all of  this they they don't quite know the the  language of ancient sumer just what kind  of a language it was like  it seems to be an isolated language  although some rabid nationalists try to  claim it as the  ancestor of their own language in  different language groups uh but soon  thereafter this semitic language the  sumerian or excuse me acadian or acadian  took over and this is also the ancestor  of hebrew and aramaic and and arabic and  so forth and so on so so the study of  arabic today for me and greek and if i  ever get into hebrew but much much  smaller number of speakers is a form of  discovering a little bit about  that part of the world  uh the history that preceded  uh  islam and then since islam and a lot of  the culture  that developed after  and i'm curious to understand you know  what are the different influences that  led to the wonderful examples of arabic  culture that are on display for example  that i visited in spain in southern  spain so these are all kinds of things  that i want to explore and i want to  explore  going further east iranian and  eventually the languages of india and so  forth and so on  but  so languages are a form of exploration  uh of discovery  learning about different people  but uh the other thing of course about  christmas day is that it is for many  christians a religious day  and the message is one of peace  peace humility  helping those less fortunate than you  turning the other cheek forgiveness  tolerance a lot of qualities  that seem to be absent  today in politics  absent today  in the way different religious  groups  interact  it's almost as if religion has become  more a matter of identity  my group is better than your group  and this leads to violence and  intolerance and we do see this in the  middle east  and so i guess the message  other today other than to encourage  people to learn languages as a means of  discovering more about  human history human beings people who  share the planet with us while we are on  this planet  other than that positive i want to  inject some positive motivation if  possible i mean people are all motivated  differently  in favor of language learning  but also  to suggest that  hopefully we can get back to the  fundamental  message of most religions and  most religions  you know while they have a sense of  identity there and symbolism and  ceremonies at the core of most religions  are  a set of fairly universal values and it  seems that  maybe it's always been there but there  seems to be an element of nastiness and  intolerance that has  has come to dominate  uh relationships between different  national groups religions and so forth  and so on so  hopefully today is a day that we think  what might be possible if people  actually followed the moral teachings of  their religions  and i should add those people who don't  believe in religions such as me  okay i have no opinion on the existence  or non-existence of of a god i simply  don't know  but  i guide my self in terms of moral values  and how to behave  based on what i think  how i think i would like to be treated  by others  and i think that basic  concept exists in all religions and so  if we can be  perhaps guided more by the old golden  rule  things might improve but i don't hold  out any hope and so i continue learning  my languages bye for now  [Music]  you
 

